# Settings Screen — Structure Review

## Problem

You have three distinct components (`SettingsItem`, `SignOutButton`, and `Settings`) plus all their styles co-located in a single file. This works for a first pass, but it creates problems as the codebase grows:

- `SettingsItem` and `SignOutButton` are reusable UI components that other screens may need. Keeping them locked inside `Settings.tsx` makes them inaccessible without duplication.
- The single `StyleSheet` mixes concerns — it contains styles for three different components, making it harder to maintain each one independently.
- The file becomes harder to navigate and test as more items or variants are added.

## Recommended Structure

Split each component into its own file under the `settings` directory:

```
src/screens/settings/
  Settings.tsx              ← screen-level component only
  components/
    SettingsItem.tsx
    SignOutButton.tsx
```

---

## Refactored Files

### `src/screens/settings/components/SettingsItem.tsx`

```tsx
import React from 'react';
import { TouchableOpacity, Text, StyleSheet } from 'react-native';

type Props = {
  label: string;
  onPress: () => void;
};

export const SettingsItem = ({ label, onPress }: Props) => (
  <TouchableOpacity style={styles.item} onPress={onPress}>
    <Text style={styles.label}>{label}</Text>
  </TouchableOpacity>
);

const styles = StyleSheet.create({
  item: { padding: 16 },
  label: { fontSize: 16 },
});
```

---

### `src/screens/settings/components/SignOutButton.tsx`

```tsx
import React from 'react';
import { TouchableOpacity, Text, StyleSheet } from 'react-native';

type Props = {
  onPress: () => void;
};

export const SignOutButton = ({ onPress }: Props) => (
  <TouchableOpacity style={styles.button} onPress={onPress}>
    <Text style={styles.label}>Sign out</Text>
  </TouchableOpacity>
);

const styles = StyleSheet.create({
  button: { padding: 16, backgroundColor: 'red' },
  label: { color: 'white' },
});
```

---

### `src/screens/settings/Settings.tsx`

```tsx
import React from 'react';
import { View, StyleSheet } from 'react-native';
import { SettingsItem } from './components/SettingsItem';
import { SignOutButton } from './components/SignOutButton';

export const Settings = () => (
  <View style={styles.container}>
    <SettingsItem label="Profile" onPress={() => {}} />
    <SettingsItem label="Notifications" onPress={() => {}} />
    <SignOutButton onPress={() => {}} />
  </View>
);

const styles = StyleSheet.create({
  container: { flex: 1 },
});
```

---

## Summary of Changes

| Before | After |
|---|---|
| All three components in one file | Each component in its own file |
| One merged `StyleSheet` | Each file owns only its own styles |
| Subcomponents not reusable elsewhere | `SettingsItem` and `SignOutButton` can be imported from anywhere |
| `Settings.tsx` hard to scan | `Settings.tsx` only contains the screen layout |

## One Optional Addition

If you anticipate many settings-area components, add a barrel export to simplify imports:

### `src/screens/settings/components/index.ts`

```ts
export { SettingsItem } from './SettingsItem';
export { SignOutButton } from './SignOutButton';
```

Then `Settings.tsx` imports become:

```tsx
import { SettingsItem, SignOutButton } from './components';
```
